5 true] [17592186403382 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403382 1084 #uuid "dbb53620-476d-5ae9-ad63-21ba59d0bc6a" 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403384 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403383 1069 "q-70769" 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403383 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403383 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\nII. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\nIII. $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$" 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403383 1072 #uuid "3692464e-f017-5c85-befd-6c0f45c59ad9" 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403383 1074 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ does **not** lie within this range.   $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403384 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403385 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403384 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403386 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403384 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403387 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403384 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403388 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403384 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403389 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403384 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403384 1077 "selection" 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403384 1079 #edb/ref 17592186403386 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403384 1080 #uuid "5374ccff-1aaf-5e15-ac23-13d1d380d377" 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403385 1081 "II and III only" 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403385 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403385 1084 #uuid "5f6ed4e6-674a-582a-a23b-375ebe06e8c3" 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403386 1081 "I only" 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403386 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403386 1084 #uuid "89701089-790b-5af5-b05f-fbbc42e2e82d" 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403387 1081 "III only" 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403387 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403387 1084 #uuid "79b59ff2-8782-539d-b7b2-9e27c2434930" 13194139533492 17592186045585 true] [17592186403388